<h1>
                    Command-Line Interfaces and System Utilities
                </h1>
<p>
                    Learn how to interact with operating systems
                    through text commands and use essential utilities
                    to manage, monitor, secure, and maintain computers.
                </p>
</header>
<div class="book-content">
<!-- INTRODUCTION -->
<section class="chapter" id="introduction">
<h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>
                        A Command-Line Interface (CLI) allows users to
                        communicate with an operating system by typing
                        text commands rather than selecting graphical
                        buttons and menus.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Command-line tools are useful for managing files,
                        checking system information, running programs,
                        configuring networks, and performing administrative
                        tasks.
                    </p>
<p>
                        System utilities are tools that help maintain,
                        monitor, protect, and improve computer performance.
                        Together, CLI tools and system utilities are
                        essential skills in Information Technology.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- LEARNING OBJECTIVES -->
<section class="chapter" id="learning-objectives">
<h2>Learning Objectives</h2>
<p>After studying this topic, learners should be able to:</p>
<ol>
<li>Define a command-line interface.</li>
<li>Explain the purpose of command-line tools.</li>
<li>Identify the parts of a command.</li>
<li>Differentiate CLI from GUI.</li>
<li>Recognize Windows and Linux commands.</li>
<li>Understand file and directory management.</li>
<li>Explain the purpose of system utilities.</li>
<li>Identify system monitoring tools.</li>
<li>Understand basic networking commands.</li>
<li>Recognize command-line security practices.</li>
<li>Apply commands to practical situations.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 1 -->
<section class="chapter" id="meaning-of-cli">
<h2>1. Meaning of Command-Line Interface</h2>
<p>
                        A Command-Line Interface is a text-based method
                        of interacting with a computer. Users enter
                        commands in a terminal or command window.
                    </p>
<p>
                        The operating system processes the command
                        and displays a result. A command may show
                        information, open a program, create a folder,
                        or perform another task.
                    </p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>dir</code></pre>
<p>
                        In Windows Command Prompt, this command displays
                        the files and folders in the current directory.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 2 -->
<section class="chapter" id="history-of-cli">
<h2>2. History and Development of CLI</h2>
<p>
                        Early computers relied heavily on text-based
                        instructions because graphical interfaces
                        were not yet common.
                    </p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Early computing:</strong> Text commands were widely used.</li>
<li><strong>1960s–1970s:</strong> Mainframe and Unix systems advanced command-based computing.</li>
<li><strong>1980s:</strong> DOS became a popular command environment on personal computers.</li>
<li><strong>1990s onward:</strong> Graphical interfaces spread, but CLI tools remained important.</li>
<li><strong>Present:</strong> Modern shells support automation, development, and administration.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 3 -->
<section class="chapter" id="how-cli-works">
<h2>3. How a CLI Works</h2>
<p>
                        A CLI accepts text instructions and passes
                        them to a command interpreter, commonly called a shell.
                    </p>
<ol>
<li>The user opens a terminal.</li>
<li>The user types a command.</li>
<li>The shell interprets the command.</li>
<li>The operating system performs the operation.</li>
<li>The terminal displays output or an error.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>ipconfig</code></pre>
<p>
                        This Windows command displays network configuration
                        information, including IP addresses and adapter details.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 4 -->
<section class="chapter" id="parts-of-command">
<h2>4. Parts of a Command</h2>
<p>
                        Commands can contain a command name,
                        options, arguments, and file paths.
                    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Part</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Command name</td>
<td>Instruction to execute</td>
<td>dir</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Option</td>
<td>Changes command behavior</td>
<td>/s</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Argument</td>
<td>Information passed to a command</td>
<td>Documents</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Path</td>
<td>File or folder location</td>
<td>C:\Users\Student</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>dir /s C:\Users\Student\Documents</code></pre>
<p>
                        The command is <code>dir</code>,
                        the option is <code>/s</code>,
                        and the remaining text identifies the directory.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 5 -->
<section class="chapter" id="cli-vs-gui">
<h2>5. CLI vs. GUI</h2>
<p>
                        A CLI uses typed commands, while a
                        Graphical User Interface (GUI) uses visual
                        controls such as icons, windows, and menus.
                    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>CLI</th>
<th>GUI</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Interaction</td>
<td>Typed commands</td>
<td>Mouse, touch, visual controls</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Appearance</td>
<td>Text-based</td>
<td>Graphical</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Learning</td>
<td>Requires command knowledge</td>
<td>Often easier for beginners</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Automation</td>
<td>Command scripts</td>
<td>Graphical automation tools</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Common use</td>
<td>Administration</td>
<td>Everyday computer tasks</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre><code>mkdir School</code></pre>
<p>
                        This creates a folder named School,
                        similar to selecting New Folder through
                        a graphical file manager.
                    </p>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 6 -->
<section class="chapter" id="command-line-environments">
<h2>6. Command-Line Environments</h2>
<p>
                        A command-line environment provides a place
                        where users enter commands and receive results.
                    </p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Command Prompt:</strong> Traditional Windows command interpreter.</li>
<li><strong>PowerShell:</strong> Shell and scripting environment for administration.</li>
<li><strong>Bash:</strong> Widely used shell on Linux and Unix-like systems.</li>
<li><strong>Zsh:</strong> Shell used on Unix-like systems, including macOS.</li>
<li><strong>Terminal applications:</strong> Programs that provide access to command-line shells.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 7 -->
<section class="chapter" id="windows-command-prompt">
<h2>7. Windows Command Prompt</h2>
<p>
                        Windows Command Prompt, or CMD, is a
                        command-line interpreter included in Windows.
                    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Command</th><th>Function</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>dir</td><td>Lists files and folders</td></tr>
<tr><td>cd</td><td>Changes directory</td></tr>
<tr><td>cls</td><td>Clears the screen</td></tr>
<tr><td>mkdir</td><td>Creates a directory</td></tr>
<tr><td>rmdir</td><td>Removes a directory</td></tr>
<tr><td>copy</td><td>Copies files</td></tr>
<tr><td>move</td><td>Moves files</td></tr>
<tr><td>ipconfig</td><td>Displays network configuration</td></tr>
<tr><td>hostname</td><td>Displays the computer name</td></tr>
<tr><td>help</td><td>Displays help information</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<pre><code>mkdir IT_Project
cd IT_Project</code></pre>
</section>
<!-- LESSON 8 -->
<section class="chapter" id="windows-powershell">
<h2>8. Windows PowerShell</h2>
<p>
                        PowerShell is a Microsoft command shell and scripting
                        language designed for system administration and automation.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Unlike traditional Command Prompt,
                        PowerShell works with structured objects.
                    </p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Command</th><th>Function</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Get-Help</td><td>Displays help</td></tr>
<tr><td>Get-Process</td><td>Lists running processes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Get-Service</td><td>Displays system services</td></tr>
<tr><td>Get-Location</td><td>Shows current location</td></tr>
